A||||9||SilverFieroV6||Vacume leak help||||5||
Z||000000||SilverFieroV6||08-08-2001||04:57 AM||||I belive that i have a vacume leak somewhere on the car.  Yesterday, i was coming to a stop at a light and my car wouldnt drop below 3500rpm.  I puled over and looked at the car, but didnt see anything unusual.  I limped the car home a few miles and checked for codes.  I got code 44, lean exhaust i talked with a few people who said its a vacume leak.  I checked all the hoses and what not but dont see anything disconnected.  Does anyone know of an easy way to find where the leak would be?<BR>Thanks<BR>Sam<P>------------------<BR>Gold 86 SE V6 4sp daily driver<BR>Silver 85 SE V6 auto ( actually running now)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||08-08-2001||06:43 AM||||Check the egr tube- they're very prone to cracking.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||08-08-2001||07:47 AM||||I agree with Phil.  It's is very likely a cracked EGR tube.<P>Since I don't know what your mechanical aptitude is I'll try to be very specific.  Forgive me if you already know this.<P>The EGR valve sits on the exhaust crossover pipe near the coil.  There is a tube that is bolted between the side of the EGR valve and the underside of the red upper intake plenum.  The original tube is made of corrugated steel and is covered with an insulating blanket wrap.  With age this tube developes cracks and allows air to be sucked into the intake manifold downstream of the throttle body.  This is called un-metered air because the TPS (Throttle position sensor) does not know it is getting into the engine.<P>The Oxygen sensor senses the lean exhaust due to the extra air and calls for more fuel from the injectors to keep the exhaust clean.  Hence the raised idle speed.<P>How do you check to see if the EGR tube is cracked?  With the engine running at idle (wherever that may be in the current condition) wiggle the EGR tube to see if it has an effect on idle speed.  You can also spray some WD-40 between the tube and its insulating blanket to see if it has an effect on idle speed.  If it does then the tube is likely cracked.<P>Replacing the tube is not all that hard to do but getting to the bolts underneath the plenum is a bit tight.  I use a 1/4" ratchet with an extension to get them.  The bolts are around 10mm or so.<P>OEM replacement tubes are pretty expensive.  Aftermarket ones aren't much cheaper but are usually better constructed.  The tubes from older 2.8L engines from Pontiac 6000 and Camaros and Firebirds will fit if you elongate the holes in one of the flanges.  They can be gotten from donor cars for 5 to 10 dollars.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||08-08-2001||09:52 AM||||all those skinny black plastic tubes running all over the place are vacuum lines too - and dont forget the ones running to the cruise control stuff on the rear left side of the engine compartment (if you have cruise).<P> some of the plastic ones on the engine run under the plenium on the V6 where they are impossible to see without using a dental mirror and flashlite, while standing on your head - trace all the vacuum lines out from end to end - if you are idling that high it sound like one is completely broken or has come off at one end.<P> or the EGR tube is cracked like others have posted above.<P>  or it could be your throttle butterfly is stuck open too.||||reg||10
Z||000004||SilverFieroV6||08-08-2001||10:37 AM||||Thanks for the input.Carrolles  thanks for the step by step instructions, you saved me from asking what im sure alot of people would have thought was a stupid question.    I am going to go out at look at the car in a little while if i can get the energy to do so as its about 100 degrees here.<BR>Thanks<BR>Sam||||reg||1
Z||000005||SilverFieroV6||08-11-2001||06:40 PM||||I finaly got out and loked at the car today.  My throttle was stuck open.  Apparently the air filter was so old that it actually ripped and a piece of the cloth was stuck in the throttle butterfly.  <BR>Any suggestions for a new air filter,  also the metal area that holds the filer is rusted at the bottom, should I replace that why I am at pep boys?<BR>Thanks <BR>Sam<p>[This message has been edited by SilverFieroV6 (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||08-11-2001||07:45 PM||||Just vacuum the loose rust out, stuff a rag into the intake, and take a wire brush to the rest. Vacuum ALL of the dust out, then making sure that the intake is plugged, fire in a coat of anti-rust paint. Let that harden fully, then slap in a new OEM filter. Done!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||rodmcneill||08-11-2001||08:52 PM||||Damn! that must have been a REALLY old air filter if it was coming apart. There is something to be said for preventive maintenance.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||08-11-2001||09:09 PM||||Oh, man.... that just can't be fixed.... no way.... better to let it float down the river and sink in the bay.<P>kidding.... I used to live just down 9 from T.R.<P>If there's a hole in the can, epoxy some thin metal over it after cleaning away the rust. then paint it like they said above. If the hole is in a spot where the filter hits it then put the patch on the outside of the can.||||reg||1
Z||000009||SilverFieroV6||08-12-2001||12:23 AM||||Thanks mach  looks like I am going to have to do that after work tommorow.  Thankfully there is no rust holes in it.<BR>Rod I bought the car like 2months ago, and thats prob the one small thing that I havent checked.  I guess tha lady who owned the car before me didn;t touch anything on the car.<P>Theogre  where abouts, I am basically on the bay just before seaside off rt 37.<BR>Thanks<BR>Sam<P>heres a few pics of the filter <A HREF="http://www.fbody.com/members/redredconv/Mvc-028s.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fbody.com/members/redredconv/Mvc-028s.jpg</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fbody.com/members/redredconv/Mvc-029s.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fbody.com/members/redredconv/Mvc-029s.jpg</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fbody.com/members/redredconv/Mvc-030s.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fbody.com/members/redredconv/Mvc-030s.jpg</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by SilverFieroV6 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
